.TH IMUD\-CAL 8 "2026-07-27" "imud 1.7" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
imud\-cal \- IMU calibration tool for imud
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B imud-cal
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-output\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.I mode
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B imud-cal
performs interactive sensor calibration and writes the result to a JSON
calibration file read by
.BR imud (8)
at startup.
.PP
Each mode operates the hardware directly (without the daemon running) and
guides the operator through a collection procedure via stdout prompts.
Results are merged into the calibration file: existing sections for other
modes are preserved.
.PP
Run
.B imud-cal
once after installing the hardware.
Re-run after physically repositioning the sensor or after a hard reset
that clears non-volatile sensor registers.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B gyro
Gyroscope bias capture.
Keep the board completely still during collection.
The bias vector is averaged over the settle window and written to the
calibration file.
Takes approximately 5\(en10 seconds.
.TP
.B accel
Accelerometer 6-position calibration.
Place the board in each of the six cardinal orientations
(+X, \-X, +Y, \-Y, +Z, \-Z face up) as prompted.
Fits a scale-and-offset model that corrects for sensitivity and misalignment.
Takes approximately 2\(en5 minutes.
.TP
.B mag
Magnetometer hard/soft-iron calibration.
Perform at least two slow 360\(de rotations about each axis in the local
magnetic environment (away from large metal objects).
Fits an ellipsoid model that corrects for hard-iron offsets and soft-iron
distortion.
Takes approximately 3\(en10 minutes.
.IP
The collection is guided: a live bar shows the heading circle split into
24 sectors (\(oq#\(cq covered, \(oq.\(cq still needed, \(oqo\(cq the
direction currently pointed), with a running coverage percentage and, once
enough samples exist, the live field radius and RMS fit residual. When
every sector is covered the tool sounds a bell and says so \(em one more
circle then improves the fit; press Ctrl-C to finish.
.TP
.B characterize
Allan-variance noise analysis of a stationary capture (requires
.BI \-\-from \ FILE\c
).
Computes per-axis gyro noise density, gyro bias instability, and
accelerometer noise density, and writes them to cal.json's
\(dqnoise\(dq section as informational per-unit characterization \(em a record
of what the silicon actually does.
These numbers never feed the filter: the MEKF always uses the tuned
mekf_* values in
.BR imud.conf (5),
which are held deliberately above the measured sensor floor.
Record overnight with [capture] enabled for a trustworthy bias-instability
floor; shorter records yield upper bounds and say so.
Offline: never touches the sensors.
.TP
.B fit-temp
Linear gyro-bias/temperature fit from a warm-up capture (requires
.BI \-\-from \ FILE\c
).
Record from cold boot until the die temperature levels out (a span of
several \(deC is required); the daemon then subtracts the fitted
temperature term from the gyro before fusion.
Offline: never touches the sensors.
.TP
.B fit\-ra
Check the MEKF's accelerometer measurement model against a rough\-water
capture (requires
.BI \-\-from \ FILE\c
).
Replays the capture through the filter exactly as the daemon would and
reports the gravity\-direction residual, its correlation time, and the mean
normalised innovation squared (NIS) \(em the same quantity the daemon
publishes live as
.BR nis_accel .
Unlike every other mode this one writes nothing: the value it comments on
lives in
.BR imud.conf (5)'s
[fusion] section, not in cal.json, because it is filter tuning rather than a
property of the sensor.
.IP
A mean NIS of roughly 10\(en30 is normal in a seaway. The residual there is
wave\-orbital and strongly time\-correlated, so no single white\-noise value
describes it; raising
.B mekf_accel_noise
until NIS reaches 1 makes the innovations look consistent but measurably
degrades attitude accuracy. A large residual
.I mean
(as opposed to variance) instead indicates a wrong mount rotation or accel
calibration, which no noise setting can fix.
Requires a mag calibration in cal.json \(em without heading updates the
covariance diverges and the analysis is refused.
Offline: never touches the sensors.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config \ PATH
Config file to use.
Defaults to
.I /etc/imud/imud.conf
(then
.IR ~/.config/imud/imud.conf ).
Determines which sensor driver, I2C bus, and GPIO lines to use.
.TP
.BI \-\-output \ PATH
Write calibration output to
.I PATH
instead of the path set by
.B calibration.file
in the config.
.TP
.BI \-\-from \ FILE
The .imucap capture to analyze (required by
.BR characterize ,
.BR fit-temp ,
and
.BR fit\-ra ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/imud/cal.json
Default calibration output path.
.TP
.I /etc/imud/imud.conf
Default configuration file.
.SH NOTES
The daemon
.RB ( imud )
and
.B imud-cal
must not run simultaneously; both open the same I2C device.
Stop the daemon before running calibration:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo systemctl stop imud
sudo imud-cal --config /etc/imud/imud.conf mag
sudo systemctl start imud
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
on configuration or hardware error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR imud (8),
.BR imud.conf (5)
.SH AUTHORS
Richard Simpson
